{"entity":{"id":"hb:la-tene-culture@-699","name":"La Tène culture","dataset":"basemaps","kind":"culture"},"territories":[{"id":358,"name":"La Tène culture","set_id":"hb_-700","class":"culture","valid_from":-699,"valid_to":-499,"snapshot_held":1,"area_km2":28109.318907834688,"border_precision":1,"capital_name":null,"sovereign":null,"sovereign_note":null,"modern_name":"France","min_lon":1.303663254593226,"min_lat":48.188435150412715,"max_lon":5.409955549119389,"max_lat":49.66182032337883,"source_file":"world_bc700.geojson","source_row":null}],"identities":[{"qid":"Q208247","from_t":-699,"to_t":-499,"method":"name-label, undated","confidence":0.5,"label":"La Tène culture","description":"archaeological culture","inception":null,"dissolved":null,"enwiki":"La Tène culture","summary":"The La Tène culture (; French pronunciation: [la tɛn]) was a European Iron Age culture. It developed and flourished during the late Iron Age (from about 450 BC to the Roman conquest in the 1st century BC), succeeding the early Iron Age Hallstatt culture without any definite cultural break, under considerable Mediterranean influence from the Greeks in pre-Roman Gaul, the Etruscans, and the Golasecca culture, but whose artistic style nevertheless did not depend on those Mediterranean influences.\nLa Tène culture's territorial extent corresponded to what is now France, Belgium, Switzerland, Austria, England, Southern Germany, the Czech Republic, Northern Italy and Central Italy, Slovenia, Hungary and Liechtenstein, as well as adjacent parts of the Netherlands, Slovakia, Serbia, Croatia, Transylvania (western Romania), and Transcarpathia (western Ukraine). The Celtiberians of western Iberia shared many aspects of the culture, though not generally the artistic style."}],"facts":[{"qid":"Q208247","kind":"predecessor","value_qid":"Q223998","value_label":"Urnfield culture","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P155","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Urnfield culture"},{"qid":"Q208247","kind